    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d895">

                <pb facs="1406" n="1392" xml:id="pg1392"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 E.W./11–1144</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of War</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stimson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">and the Secretary of the Navy</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Forrestal</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-11-11">November 11,
                        1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: We are pleased to inform
                    you that our two Departments have joined in setting up a national office for the
                    preparation and prosecution of war crimes cases, along the same general line as
                    that of the national offices which have been established by others of the United
                        Nations.<anchor n="23" xml:id="d895-f1"/> The mission of this office will be
                    to collect evidence of cruelties, atrocities, and acts of oppression against
                    members of the United States armed forces or other Americans, including the
                    people of any dependency of the United States, such as the Philippines;
                    examination of such evidence; arranging for the apprehension and prompt trial of
                    persons against whom a prima facie case is made out, and for the execution of
                    any sentences which may be imposed.</p>
                <p>The above will not affect the handling of matters of policy regarding war crimes.
                    These will continue to be handled as heretofore.</p>
                <p>It is hoped that this action will meet with your concurrence. Your views and
                    comments on the matter would be appreciated.<anchor n="24" xml:id="d895-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry L. Stimson</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">James Forrestal</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d895-f1" n="23" xml:id="d895-f1-content">
                <p>For list of National Offices established by others of the United Nations, see <hi
                        rendition="#italic">History of the United Nations War Crimes
                    Commission</hi>, p. 121.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d895-f2" n="24" xml:id="d895-f2-content">
                <p>The Department’s concurrence was conveyed in memoranda to the War and Navy
                    Departments, dated November 7, 1944.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
